july is fast approaching the start of  flash flood season in the valley the  flood control district reminding  everybody about the dangers first don't  drive through flooded streets turn  around and don't drown cars can be swept  away quickly in fast moving water steer  clear of detention basins channels  washes and storm drains and water can  flow through these areas at up to 30  miles per hour again it only takes six  inches of rushing water to knock you  down  pool again safety is key because  flooding can happen when you least  expect it now the regional flood control  district is laying out plans to protect  you during these situations  floods can happen at any time of the  year without warning  as flash flood season rolls in clark  county is trying to stay ahead of the  game to keep southern nevada residents  safe around the valley about 75 percent  of the regional flood control district's  master plan has been completed that is  the areas in blue the red marks are the  areas still in progress  since the mid 1980's about 680 miles of  storm channels and over 100 detention  basins have been built taxpayers  spending 2.16 billion dollars and  improving more than 60 square miles of  land that have been removed from flood  zones  as the rain season approaches the race  is on to get as much of these 47  projects completed including the blue  diamond wash the meadows charleston  storm drain and the gowan el capitan on  centennial parkway drone video shot by 8  news now shows fort apache and warm  springs just one of many projects across  clark county that officials hope will  keep residents safe  tomorrow clark county officials and the  regional flood control district are  expected to give an update on all of  these projects
 

